  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nCold front will continue to push precipitation \nsouthward through this afternoon. With the upper level support \nshifting eastward, the boundary will become nearly parallel to the \nflow, slowing down as it moves south of the forecast area. This \ncould keep lingering precip chances over our WV mountain counties \novernight but the bulk of the rest of the region should be dry.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nBroad upper troughing looks to persist through the \nweekend and into next week, with temperatures returning to seasonal \nvalues for early October. The cooler temperatures will be reinforced \nby a shortwave digging through the upper trough on Sunday. This wave \ncould pull moisture, from the boundary that becomes diffuse to the \nsouth of our region after crossing today, northward, leading to some \npossible showers south and east of Pittsburgh. At this time the \nprobability of more than a few light showers/sprinkles is fairly low \n(Prob > 0.05 inches around 30% in the WV ridges). After Sunday, \nbuilding surface high pressure looks to keep the region dry for\nmuch of the rest of the week. \n\nOne thing to watch will be the potential for frost both Tuesday and \nWednesday mornings to the northeast of Pittsburgh, as well as in the \nusual Laurel Highland/northern West Virginia cold spots. The NBM \npaints a 50% to 75% chance of low temperatures colder than 37 \ndegrees north of US-422 both mornings - which would be chilly enough \nfor at least patchy frost given clear and calm overnight conditions. \nTemperatures from Wednesday on should build back towards more \nseasonal values.",
